   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Saying your name",
     "texto": [
      "To say your name, use the verb **hedder** (from «at hedde», to be called): **Jeg hedder Ana** = «My name is Ana».\n\nDanish doesn't add a word like English «my» here: the verb itself says that someone has that name.\n\nTo ask it: **Hvad hedder du?** = «What's your name?» (roughly, «what name do you have?»)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The subject can't be left out",
     "texto": [
      "In English we always say «I am from Spain», with the «I». Danish is the same: **Jeg er fra Spanien**.\n\nAnd remember: the verb doesn't change with the person. **Jeg hedder**, **du hedder**… always the same."
     ],
     "tutora": "pensando"
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Three new letters: æ, ø, å",
     "texto": [
      "Danish has three vowels that English doesn't have:\n\n- **æ** sounds like an open «e», like the «e» in «bed», but more open.\n- **ø** is like saying «e» with rounded lips, like the French «eu».\n- **å** sounds like an open «o», like the «aw» in «law».\n\nYou'll find them in words like **København** (Copenhagen) or **møde** (to meet)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
